WEBVTT - The Sound Above: Episode 1 (Sergio Pereira and Chicago Alt.NET Monologue)
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
<v Mike Hall>This is The Sound Above. I'm Mike Hall, and today I'm going back to November 19, 2011, at Software Craftsmanship North America at the Swissotel in downtown Chicago.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:22.000
<v Mike Hall>My very first UGtastic interview was with Sergio Pereira, the organizer of Chicago Alt.NET. I didn't have a studio rig or a camera crew that day.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:34.000
<v Mike Hall>I just sat down with Sergio in the hallway, flipped open my 15-inch unibody MacBook Pro, opened Photo Booth, and recorded using the laptop's built-in camera and microphone.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:46.000
<v Mike Hall>There is a personal reason I asked Sergio to sit down first. Chicago Alt.NET was the first user group I ever attended, and the first user group where I ever stood up and gave a talk.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:58.000
<v Mike Hall>My first meeting was Wednesday, August 13, 2008. I rode the Metra Union Pacific Northwest train in from Crystal Lake to see a demo of ThoughtWorks Studios' Cruise continuous integration server.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:10.000
<v Mike Hall>And the person who recorded, edited, and posted the video of that August 2008 meeting online, including my questions from the audience, was Sergio Pereira. Three years later at SCNA, we traded places, and I recorded him.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:22.000
<v Mike Hall>Right at the start of the recording, I ask Sergio what Chicago Alt.NET is. He refuses to give a neat ideological definition, and instead describes the people in the room and what they were trying to do.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:34.000
<v Mike Hall>They were working developers who used .NET every day, liked .NET, and wanted to improve their craft and the quality of the software they wrote.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:46.000
<v Mike Hall>Back on April 10, 2007, David Laribee had published the Alt.NET Manifesto, and that October developers gathered in Austin for the first Alt.NET Open Spaces. Sergio started Chicago Alt.NET in late 2007 with Derek Lakin and Uriah, and later ran it with Eduardo.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:58.000
<v Mike Hall>They met on the second Wednesday of every month at 6:30 PM at Redpoint Technologies in the Sears Tower, where Redpoint provided the room and sponsored the pizza every month.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:10.000
<v Mike Hall>A typical meeting had about twenty-five people, which Sergio points out is a great size for someone who has never spoken at a user group before. He encouraged members to bring the talks they had already given to their teams at work.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:24.000
<v Mike Hall>On November 12, 2008, just three months after my first meeting, I stood up at Redpoint Technologies and gave my first presentation on the NHaml view engine, right alongside Adam Tybor presenting on Spark.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:36.000
<v Mike Hall>The question I want to follow in this rewatch is straightforward: What made Chicago Alt.NET a place where people kept coming back, and how did that room connect to the wider Chicago software community?

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:50.000
<v Mike Hall>Sergio says the group was never only about Microsoft or .NET. It was about software development: NHibernate, CodeSharp, IronRuby, test-driven development, behavior-driven development, and Kanban.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:03:04.000
<v Mike Hall>In his April 2009 Wrox book, Ruby on Rails for Microsoft Developers, Antonio Cangiano documented how Alt.NET developers looking to Castle MonoRail and Rails helped push Microsoft from WebForms postbacks toward ASP.NET MVC and shipping jQuery in Visual Studio.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:18.000
<v Mike Hall>In my March 12, 2009 blog recap, I wrote about one of Sergio's four-chair Fishbowl nights debating Domain-Driven Design with Adam Tybor and discussing the db4o object database. That same spring, a Twitter debate with Sergio prompted me to build twatio.com on ASP.NET MVC to test follower overlap empirically.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:34.000
<v Mike Hall>And when we recently resurrected the 2010 to 2012 Ruby on Rails repository, just3ws/ccs, that powered chicagocodecamp.com, and cross-referenced the Software Craftsmanship Manifesto ledger, we found Sergio right in the evidence.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:48.000
<v Mike Hall>Sergio signed the Manifesto in 2009 as Signatory 592 from Grayslake, Illinois, spoke at the May 2012 camp, and was the number two code contributor to our Rails app with twenty-four commits in Ruby, Haml, and Sass alongside my 764 commits.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:04:02.000
<v Mike Hall>Let's play the 2011 conversation with Sergio Pereira. Listen for how he describes the twenty-five-person room, cultivating internal speakers, experimenting with fishbowl debates, and keeping community events open across platforms.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:16.000
<v Mike Hall>Having the searchable forty-four-turn transcript open beside this recording is the result of a fifteen-year arc: from my 2014 many-hands-make-light-work crowdsourcing repo, through our five-Raspberry-Pi whisper.cpp Fluffle cluster in 2023, to local Apple Silicon transcription today.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:30.000
<v Mike Hall>On line 145 of the transcript, Sergio says: "That's the place that they can come, see those things, and judge for themselves if it's going to work for them or not."

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:44.000
<v Mike Hall>Whether it was drag-and-drop WebForms wizards in 2008 or generative AI code synthesis in 2026, tools change fast, and no vendor default does your engineering judgment for you. We still need rooms where working developers share real work and verify new tools together.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:58.000
<v Mike Hall>Thank you to Sergio Pereira for building that room in Chicago, for writing code with us on Chicago Code Camp, and for sitting down with me at SCNA 2011 for the very first UGtastic interview.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:15.000
<v Mike Hall>The full interactive transcript and all archival links are on the Episode 1 page at just3ws.com, and you can support the work at just3ws.com/support. Next time, Ray Hightower talks about ChicagoRuby and grassroots hospitality. Until then, keep your feedback loops fast, take care of each other, and keep making room to learn.
